<p>An isolation level says how one transaction may observe and interfere with another. It decides whether a second read can change, whether an update waits for a writer, and whether the database may reject a transaction whose decisions cannot fit a serial order.</p>
<p>The name alone is too little to design against. PostgreSQL Repeatable Read and MySQL InnoDB Repeatable Read both give ordinary reads a stable view, but a locking read can make them behave differently. An application that treats either as “everything uses the state I first read” can make the wrong decision.</p>
<p>We will follow class 21’s last place from <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/">Making concurrent changes correct</a>, then use its two instructors to expose a decision spanning different rows. PostgreSQL observations come from native <strong>18.6</strong>. The selected InnoDB comparisons come from the <strong>MySQL 8.4 manual</strong>; no MySQL server was run. The figures are teaching models, with the engine observations checked separately.</p>

<nav aria-label="In this article"><ol>{sections.map(([id,title])=><li><a href={`#${id}`}>{title}</a></li>)}</ol></nav>

<h2 id="turn-an-isolation-name-into-a-contract">Turn an isolation name into a contract</h2>
<p>Class 21 has capacity one, one remaining place and no reservations. Accepting a booking must reduce remaining places by one and record its owner in the same transaction. Every committed state must keep remaining places plus reservations equal to capacity. Mina and Tariq may both ask; only one may succeed.</p>
<p>Suppose Tariq reads one remaining place. What entitles him to act on it? An ordinary read may show a permitted old version while Mina changes the current row. Keeping Tariq’s read stable does not necessarily make the place his.</p>
<p>Read the contract for the exact engine, version and statement. Extract three answers: when an ordinary read chooses its view; what a conflicting locking read or change waits for, returns or rejects; and what successful commit promises about the combined transactions. Record the actual transaction setting too. PostgreSQL normally defaults to Read Committed; InnoDB normally defaults to Repeatable Read. A connection can override either.</p>
<p>A <strong>snapshot</strong> is the view of committed work that a read is allowed to see. <strong>Snapshot isolation</strong> gives a transaction a stable snapshot and arbitrates conflicting writes, but does not by itself rule out every incompatible combination of decisions. <strong>Serialisability</strong> requires committed work to have an effect equivalent to some execution of those transactions one at a time.</p>
<p>These guarantees have premises. Each transaction must preserve the application’s rule when run alone, and the protection must cover every relevant writer. The parent article develops that <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/#choose-protection-for-the-invariant-you-actually-have">choice of protection</a>. Here we will read and challenge the engine behaviour on which the choice depends.</p>

<h2 id="establish-when-the-transaction-sees-a-change">Establish when the transaction sees a change</h2>
<p>In Figure 1, A reads one place, B takes it and commits, then A reads again. Step through <em>Read Committed</em>, then switch to <em>Repeatable Read</em> and compare A’s second answer.</p>
<IsolationFigure scene="visibility" number={1} />
<p>The native PostgreSQL check returned one then zero at Read Committed, and one then one at Repeatable Read. Read Committed gave each command a new snapshot. Repeatable Read kept the snapshot established by the transaction’s first query or data-changing command. The remaining-place row really had changed in both runs.</p>
<p>Now choose <em>Repeatable Read: first read later</em>. A begins before B commits but performs its first data query afterwards. It reads zero. The native check returned the same result: <code>BEGIN</code> alone had not fixed the data snapshot. A subsequent change made by A itself was visible to A. A transaction’s own changes are an exception to the earlier snapshot, not evidence that its ordinary reads now follow every other writer.</p>
<p>For ordinary consistent reads, the <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-consistent-read.html">MySQL 8.4 contract</a> has the same useful distinction: a fresh view per read at Read Committed, and a view established by the first consistent read at Repeatable Read, with own changes visible. Check transaction-start options as well; InnoDB can explicitly request a consistent snapshot.</p>
<p>A stable snapshot is useful for a report whose several queries must describe one view. It is not a reservation for a later change. Keeping old versions available also has a cost when a transaction stays open; <a href="/review/relational-next/physical-storage/">How stored rows become physical work</a> follows that resource cost.</p>

<h2 id="read-and-update-can-consult-different-states">Read and update can consult different states</h2>
<p>B has read one place. A changes the row to zero but has not committed. B now asks for that row with <code>SELECT … FOR UPDATE</code>, a locking read. It needs protection against a competing writer, so it cannot simply finish by returning whichever old version an ordinary read could use.</p>
<p>Play Figure 2 with <em>PostgreSQL Read Committed: lock</em>. Watch the committed row, A’s pending change and B’s result separately. Then compare PostgreSQL Repeatable Read.</p>
<IsolationFigure scene="locking" number={2} />
<p>In both native runs B waited while A held the row. After A committed, Read Committed returned zero from the locking read. Repeatable Read failed with SQLSTATE <code>40001</code>: PostgreSQL would not let B lock the row changed since its snapshot. Rolling back and starting a new transaction is a different operation from refreshing one earlier read.</p>
<p>At Read Committed an updater can likewise wait for a concurrent writer and reconsider its condition against the updated row. Express the capacity condition inside the change:</p>
<Sql code={guarded} label="Check and decrement the same row, in one PostgreSQL statement" />
<p>Select <em>PostgreSQL Read Committed: guarded update</em>. A takes the place first; B waits, then changes zero rows because <code>remaining &gt; 0</code> no longer holds. The native worksheet observed that outcome. The corresponding Repeatable Read attempt waited and then received <code>40001</code>. Neither is permission to insert B’s reservation. In the complete booking, only a successful decrement feeds the reservation insert, and insertion failure rolls the decrement back, as in the <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/#express-a-change-and-its-condition-together">parent’s complete statement</a>.</p>
<p>Now choose <em>InnoDB Repeatable Read: mixed reads</em>. Its documented locking read uses current data even though an ordinary consistent read keeps the older view. B can read one ordinarily, acquire the current zero through a locking read after A commits, then read one ordinarily again. This is a documentation-derived illustration, not a measured MySQL result. The <a href="https://dev.mysql.com/doc/refman/8.4/en/innodb-transaction-isolation-levels.html">8.4 manual warns about mixing these views</a>; a successful locking read does not make every later ordinary query current.</p>
<p>A further PostgreSQL trap appears even at Read Committed: putting a count and a locking clause in the same command does not give the count a fresh snapshot after the wait. <a href="/review/relational-next/locking/#give-the-invariant-a-stable-coordination-row">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> shows the observed result and constructs the separate-command protocol. It also owns lock modes, absent rows, acquisition order and deadlocks. A lock’s coverage and a read’s visibility are separate parts of the argument.</p>

<h2 id="a-consistent-snapshot-can-still-permit-an-invalid-combination">A consistent snapshot can still permit an invalid combination</h2>
<p>Mina and Tariq are the two instructors. At least one must remain available. A departure transaction counts the available instructors and leaves only if the count is greater than one. Each transaction is correct by itself: the first person leaves, the second sees one and stays.</p>
<p>At PostgreSQL Repeatable Read, both can count two before either changes anything. Each then changes its own row. Figure 3 traces these observations. Watch the two dependencies appear before either commit.</p>
<IsolationFigure scene="staffing" number={3} />
<p>The native Repeatable Read run accepted both departures and finished with zero available. Neither transaction overwrote the other’s row. Preventing two writers from successfully replacing the same row did not protect a decision based on both rows.</p>
<p>The arrows explain why the result is not serialisable. A read Tariq as available, so A needs to precede B’s departure in any serial explanation. B read Mina as available, so B needs to precede A. Those requirements form a cycle, <strong>A → B → A</strong>. Neither one-at-a-time order can produce both observed decisions.</p>
<p>The same question applies when a query selects a changing set. For Figure 4, use the locking article’s counted-ownership design: the class has capacity one, and free capacity is calculated from reservation rows. There is no remaining counter in this variant. Both transactions read an empty set of reservations for class 21, then insert different owners.</p>
<IsolationFigure scene="predicate" number={4} />
<p>The native Repeatable Read run committed two bookings. A new matching row invalidated each transaction’s earlier count without replacing any row it had read. The condition <code>WHERE place_id = 21</code> is a <strong>predicate</strong>: its result is a set that an insertion can change. Repeatability of existing rows is therefore too narrow a check for this capacity rule.</p>

<h2 id="serialisability-protects-an-outcome-by-ordering-or-rejection">Serialisability protects an outcome by ordering or rejection</h2>
<p>Switch Figures 3 and 4 to <em>PostgreSQL Serializable</em>. The selected native runs let both transactions read their initial result and perform their different-row writes. A committed; B’s commit failed with <code>40001</code>. One instructor and one booking survived respectively. The figures replay that chosen outcome; another schedule can fail at another statement or choose a different victim.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL builds this protection on its snapshot behaviour, tracking read/write dependencies that could make the combination unsafe. Its <strong>predicate locks</strong>, visible as <code>SIReadLock</code> in <code>pg_locks</code>, support that detection. They do not hold every matching writer waiting. The successful writes followed by B’s rejected commit show why “the UPDATE worked” is too early to report a departure as accepted.</p>
<p>A locking implementation can prevent the incompatible progression earlier. Under MySQL 8.4 InnoDB Serializable with <code>autocommit</code> disabled, ordinary selects become shared locking reads. The staffing reads would therefore introduce lock conflicts with departures, rather than following PostgreSQL’s nonblocking read-dependency path. That is a documented mechanism comparison, not an observed MySQL staffing run. Waiting and deadlock handling still matter; an isolation name does not promise that every attempt succeeds.</p>
<p>After the known PostgreSQL abort, repeat the <em>whole</em> transaction, including the read and the decision. B’s new native attempt counted one instructor and declined Tariq’s departure. Repeating only “make Tariq unavailable” would carry the old decision into a new transaction and break the rule.</p>
<p>There is also nothing for serialisability to repair if the decision itself is wrong. We changed the query to count only the departing person and allowed departure whenever that count was positive. Two transactions run strictly one after another at Serializable then both committed, leaving nobody available. Each needed a different question: how many will remain? The contract protects correct transaction logic from incompatible interleavings; it cannot invent the application invariant.</p>
<p><a href="https://www.postgresql.org/docs/18/mvcc-serialization-failure-handling.html">PostgreSQL’s failure guidance</a> requires complete transaction retries after relevant serialisation failures. Limits, deadlines and driver behaviour belong to <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a>. A missing COMMIT reply is a different problem: it does not establish a known abort. The <a href="/review/relational-next/transactions/#losing-the-reply-does-not-prove-rollback">parent separates that unknown outcome</a>.</p>

<h2 id="a-serial-order-does-not-establish-a-real-time-order">A serial order does not establish a real-time order</h2>
<p>Serial equivalence asks whether <em>some</em> one-at-a-time order explains the committed work. A stronger contract also requires that order to respect real time: if one transaction completes before another begins, the first must come first in the serial explanation. This extra requirement is commonly called <strong>strict serialisability</strong>.</p>
<p>In Figure 5, a booking’s write completes before a later read starts. Compare an explanation that is merely serial with one that must also respect that external order.</p>
<IsolationFigure scene="ordering" number={5} />
<p>A read returning the earlier value can be explained by placing the read before the write. Serial equivalence alone admits that explanation; the completed-before-started requirement rejects it. This is a comparison of definitions, not a claim that PostgreSQL or InnoDB permits the figure’s reversed history. Google’s <a href="https://docs.cloud.google.com/spanner/docs/true-time-external-consistency">description of Spanner’s external consistency</a> explicitly distinguishes the additional ordering guarantee from serialisability.</p>
<p>For an application claim such as “after the booking acknowledgement, this next request must see it”, establish the engine’s documented ordering guarantee and the actual read path. A timestamp, one successful experiment or a serialisable setting on the writer does not establish the contract of an arbitrary later reader. A replica or explicitly older snapshot needs particular attention. <a href="/review/relational-next/replicas-and-partitions/">Operating replicas and partitioned data</a> owns those read-path choices.</p>

<h2 id="build-a-repeatable-check-and-state-what-it-proves">Build a repeatable check and state what it proves</h2>
<p>A useful check fixes the state, the transaction settings and the interesting interleaving. Use a disposable database and independently connected clients A and B. Record <code>SELECT version()</code>, <code>SHOW transaction_isolation</code>, the read/write mode and timeouts. The worksheet uses three connections: the third observes a lock wait before releasing its holder. A polling delay is not evidence of blocking; the observed server wait is.</p>
<details><summary>Complete PostgreSQL exercise schema</summary><Sql code={setup} label="Create once in an empty disposable database" /></details>
<p>Here is the staffing check in manual form. After creating the schema, use the following start in each client. The timeouts are session settings for the exercise, not production advice.</p>
<Sql code={session} label="Start both independent transactions and establish their reads" />
<p>Once both reads have returned two, run <code>UPDATE staff SET available = false WHERE person = 'Mina'</code> in A, then the corresponding update for <code>'Tariq'</code> in B. Wait for each statement to finish. Commit A, then B. Record each response and SQLSTATE. A rejected transaction must finish with rollback before the client starts again. In a new transaction B must count again, see one and leave Tariq available.</p>
<p>For another case, finish both sessions, then reset in the observer with <code>TRUNCATE reservations, places, staff</code> and repeat the two seed inserts from the setup. The reset destroys this exercise’s rows. Repeat the staffing schedule with explicit Repeatable Read to look for the counterexample. For the row-conflict check, start B, read the place, then let A decrement it without committing; send B’s guarded update, observe B waiting, and only then let A insert R1 and commit.</p>
<Sql code={check} label="Inspect the committed facts, separately from each client's response" />
<div class="table-scroll" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Expected and observed outcomes in the native PostgreSQL worksheet"><table><thead><tr><th>Case and expected distinction</th><th>Observed on PostgreSQL 18.6</th><th>Decision it supports</th></tr></thead><tbody>
 <tr><td>Two ordinary reads across a competing commit: statement versus transaction view</td><td>Read Committed: 1 then 0. Repeatable Read: 1 then 1. First data read after the commit: 0.</td><td>Do not treat a repeated value, or BEGIN time, as a reservation.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Conflicting row: locking read or guarded update after a wait</td><td>Read Committed: lock returns 0; guarded update returns no rows. Repeatable Read: each receives 40001.</td><td>Use the atomic condition and handle the actual abort contract.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Two correct staffing decisions change different rows</td><td>Repeatable Read: 0 available. Serializable: B rejected, 1 available; complete retry declines leaving.</td><td>Protect the multirow dependency, then repeat all logic after a known abort.</td></tr>
 <tr><td>Empty reservation predicate followed by two inserts</td><td>Repeatable Read: 2 bookings. Serializable: B rejected, 1 booking.</td><td>Include future matching rows in the protection argument.</td></tr>
