</tbody></table></div>
<p><a href={worksheetUrl} download="isolation-rehearsal.py">Download the repeatable worksheet</a>. It uses Python’s standard library and PostgreSQL’s <code>psql</code>, <code>createdb</code> and <code>dropdb</code> tools. Set <code>PGHOST</code>, <code>PGPORT</code> and <code>PGUSER</code> for your disposable server; optionally set <code>PG_BIN</code> to its tool directory. Set <code>REL3_ISOLATION_DB</code> to an unused name, then run <code>python3 isolation-rehearsal.py &gt; isolation-result.json</code>. It refuses an existing database, creates its own, retains statements and outputs with assertions, closes its sessions and removes only that database.</p>
<p>The recorded run used PostgreSQL 18.6 with the durable-commit safeguards on, a 12-second statement timeout, 5-second lock timeout and 45-second idle-transaction timeout. It tested the stated local schedules; it did not test MySQL, failover, external effects or every possible interleaving.</p>
<p>A counterexample can disprove a safety claim. Passing schedules cannot prove it for every execution. Finish with the contract-based argument: the guarded counter uses a condition on the row being changed and records ownership atomically; the counted-set or staffing design needs a protocol covering that full dependency, or correct logic with all relevant work under the serialisable contract. Failed attempts must not escape as accepted decisions. Recheck the cases when an engine upgrade, isolation setting, query or writer changes that argument.</p>
<p>Continue with <a href="/review/relational-next/locking/">Designing changes that coordinate with locks</a> for an explicit all-writers protocol, and <a href="/review/relational-next/request-recovery/">Recovering database requests safely</a> for bounded retries and uncertain outcomes.</p>

